From d76ffba3b1465bef25bc17090e682c19c8399141 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 20:38:56 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] l2r-local: bug 13: release chains of cells in a loop in the
 drop glue

A member release in drop glue expanded to "count == 1 -> call the member's
drop_in_place, then deallocate the member". The deallocation follows the
call, so it is not a tail call: releasing a long list at once took a stack
frame per cell and overflowed the stack (lean2rr: about 33M cells on its
1 GiB thread; 2x time and memory vs native at 10M).

No token produced inside drop glue can be reused, so there (functions
marked reussir.drop_glue) a release of a plain shared box of a named record
that can form a chain now expands to "count == 1 -> drop_and_free::<T>,
else count - 1". drop_and_free, called only on a cell whose count was read
as 1, dispatches on the arm and per arm drops the members other than its
chain members, frees the cell (its arm's size), and then releases the chain
members: the boxes whose type can hold the cell's type again (its recursive
group), wherever they sit; without one, the last managed member if it is
such a box. Chain members whose count is not 1 are only decremented; of the
others, the last is released as the final operation (a tail call), the rest
before it. So a chain is freed in a loop whichever member carries it (list
tail, left or right spine, snoc lists, mutually recursive types); a tree
recurses along its other children only. Nullary arms of taggable types are
immediates under the special-pointer-tag scheme and free nothing.

Releases outside drop glue, whose tokens may be reused, are unchanged;
atomic and regional boxes keep the old path.
